    {
      "heading": {
        "text": "4. CONTROL LOOP INTERACTION CHECK",
        "level": 1
      },
      "level": 1,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "In a DCDA plant, several control loops interact. The matrix below identifies significant interactions and assesses whether the decoupling or sequencing strategy is adequate.",
          "style": "Normal"
        }
      ],
      "tables": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "rows": [
            [
              "Loop Pair",
              "Interaction Description",
              "Interaction Severity",
              "Decoupling Strategy",
              "Adequately Addressed?"
            ],
            [
              "FIC-101 ↔ FIC-201",
              "Sulfur feed and air flow co-determine SO₂ concentration. Changing either affects the other.",
              "HIGH",
              "Governor ratio control: air leads on increase, sulfur leads on decrease (lead/lag)",
              "Yes — Governor correctly sequences the changes"
            ],
            [
              "AIC-501 ↔ AIC-502",
              "Both absorption towers share a common product acid stream; concentration changes in IPAT circuit affect FAT circuit if acid is cross-transferred.",
              "MEDIUM",
              "Independent circuits with separate dilution water controls; only product overflow interconnects them",
              "Yes — circuits are independent at FEED; confirm with detailed piping layout"
            ],
            [
              "LIC-201 ↔ PIC-202",
              "WHB drum level and steam header pressure interact: rapid steam header pressure drop causes drum level swell (shrink-and-swell phenomenon).",
              "HIGH",
              "3-element control on LIC-201 compensates for steam flow demand; PIC-202 uses steam let-down valve",
              "Yes — but shrink/swell transient must be validated by dynamic simulation"
            ],
            [
              "TIC-401 ↔ TIC-402",
              "Pass 1 outlet temperature (TIC-401 bypass) directly affects Pass 2 inlet temperature (TIC-402). These loops are strongly coupled.",
              "HIGH",
              "Interpass heat exchangers are fixed-area; bypass valves are independently controlled. Detuned PID gains required for TIC-402.",
              "MARGIN — cascade from TIC-401 output to TIC-402 setpoint should be considered to decouple the interaction"
            ],
            [
              "FIC-201 ↔ TIC-401",
              "Air flow rate affects converter inlet temperature through furnace heat release and WHB outlet conditions.",
              "MEDIUM",
              "Furnace temperature maintained by BPCS; interpass HX provides buffer",
              "Yes — adequate for regulatory control"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
